Subject: vm, api: register top-level coroutines, freeze them and resurrect
 them

`run_guest_thread` runs a whole guest-thread body on a registered
top-level coroutine driven by one OS thread that does nothing else; the
Wasm call nests its own coroutine inside and propagates its waits
outwards. The OS thread therefore holds no guest state, which lets a
freeze end it and a forked child give the coroutine a fresh thread:

- `close_gate` stops every started coroutine from being resumed or
  polled; `drain` waits until no host loop is active, then makes each
  loop capture its per-thread state and end its thread with the raw
  `exit` syscall, so no destructor runs after quiescence.
- `resurrect_all` reopens the gate and spawns one thread per registered
  coroutine that re-creates the thread's state, re-polls the pending
  wait and resumes the coroutine.

wasmer-api installs hooks that capture and restore its store-context
stack, the per-thread state the import trampolines read.

